INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Emphysema','easy','mcq','Which type of emphysema is most strongly associated with alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency?','["A. Distal acinar (paraseptal) emphysema","B. Centriacinar (centrilobular) emphysema","C. Panacinar (panlobular) emphysema","D. Irregular emphysema"]','A','Panacinar emphysema is characteristically associated with alpha-1 antitrypsin (AAT) deficiency. AAT is a major inhibitor of elastase secreted by neutrophils; its deficiency leads to unopposed protease activity causing diffuse acinar destruction, predominantly at the lung bases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Emphysema','easy','mcq','Centriacinar emphysema is most commonly associated with which of the following?','["A. Heavy cigarette smoking","B. Intravenous drug use","C. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","D. Cystic fibrosis"]','C','Centriacinar (centrilobular) emphysema involves the central or proximal parts of the acini (respiratory bronchioles) and occurs predominantly in heavy smokers, often in the upper lobes. This is the most common form of emphysema, accounting for >95% of clinically significant cases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Emphysema','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old man with severe emphysema is described as a ''''pink puffer.'''' Which combination of findings best characterizes this clinical phenotype?','["A. Chronic productive cough, cyanosis, hypercapnia, and cor pulmonale","B. Barrel chest, pursed-lip breathing, relatively normal blood gases at rest, and low diffusion capacity","C. Clubbing, hemoptysis, bronchiectasis, and recurrent infections","D. Orthopnea, bilateral crackles, elevated JVP, and peripheral edema"]','A','The "pink puffer" (typical of pure emphysema) features severe overdistention with relatively maintained oxygenation through hyperventilation, pursed-lip breathing, barrel chest, low diffusion capacity, and minimal hypoxemia at rest. In contrast, "blue bloaters" (chronic bronchitis) exhibit cyanosis, hypercapnia, and cor pulmonale.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Emphysema','medium','mcq','In emphysema, which mechanism primarily causes airflow obstruction during expiration?','["A. Increased mucus secretion plugging bronchioles","B. Loss of elastic recoil reducing radial traction on respiratory bronchioles","C. Smooth muscle hyperplasia narrowing airway lumen","D. Subepithelial fibrosis thickening the bronchial wall"]','C','In emphysema, destruction of alveolar walls leads to loss of elastic tissue. Normally, lung parenchyma provides radial traction that keeps small airways open during expiration. Loss of this elastic recoil causes respiratory bronchioles to collapse during expiration, producing functional airflow obstruction without mechanical obstruction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Emphysema','medium','mcq','Which gene locus encodes alpha-1 antitrypsin, and which allele is most strongly associated with severe emphysema?','["A. MMP12 locus on chromosome 11; null allele","B. Pi locus on chromosome 7; MM genotype","C. SERPINA1 locus on chromosome 1; SS genotype","D. Pi locus on chromosome 14; ZZ genotype"]','A','Alpha-1 antitrypsin is encoded by the proteinase inhibitor (Pi) locus on chromosome 14. Approximately 0.012% of the US population is homozygous for the Z allele (ZZ genotype), which is associated with markedly decreased serum AAT levels. More than 80% of ZZ individuals develop symptomatic panacinar emphysema.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Emphysema','medium','mcq','Which type of emphysema is associated with spontaneous pneumothorax in young adults and occurs adjacent to areas of fibrosis or near the pleura?','["A. Centriacinar emphysema","B. Panacinar emphysema","C. Distal acinar (paraseptal) emphysema","D. Irregular emphysema"]','A','Distal acinar (paraseptal) emphysema affects the distal portion of the acinus, is more prominent near the pleura and interlobular septa, and characteristically underlies many cases of spontaneous pneumothorax in young adults. It may form cyst-like structures greater than 2 cm in diameter.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Emphysema','medium','mcq','Which pulmonary function test finding is most characteristic of obstructive lung disease such as emphysema?','["A. Increased total lung capacity with normal FEV1","B. Decreased FVC with normal FEV1/FVC ratio","C. FEV1/FVC ratio less than 0.7","D. Reduced diffusion capacity with preserved FEV1/FVC ratio"]','A','Obstructive lung diseases are defined by decreased maximal airflow rates during forced expiration. An FEV1/FVC ratio of less than 0.7 indicates airflow obstruction. In restrictive disease, both FVC and FEV1 decrease proportionately, maintaining a normal ratio.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Emphysema','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old non-smoker is found to have panacinar emphysema predominantly at the lung bases. Serum levels of a specific inhibitor are markedly reduced. Which best explains the mechanism?','["A. Increased reactive oxygen species from cigarette smoke activating NRF2","B. TH2-mediated eosinophilic inflammation causing acinar destruction","C. Increased neutrophil elastase activity due to deficiency of alpha-1 antitrypsin","D. Goblet cell metaplasia obstructing respiratory bronchioles"]','B','This presentation is classic for alpha-1 antitrypsin (AAT) deficiency-related emphysema. AAT is a major inhibitor of elastase secreted by neutrophils. In its absence, even minor injuries lead to unchecked elastase activity, resulting in panacinar emphysema at the bases, occurring at a younger age and compounded by smoking.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Emphysema','hard','mcq','According to Robbins, what is the most common cause of death in patients with severe emphysema?','["A. Coronary artery disease","B. Massive pulmonary embolism","C. Malignant arrhythmia","D. Acute respiratory failure from pneumonia"]','A','According to Robbins, the most common causes of death in emphysema patients are: (1) coronary artery disease, (2) respiratory failure, (3) right-sided heart failure (cor pulmonale), and (4) massive collapse due to pneumothorax. Coronary artery disease is listed as the primary cause of death, reflecting the shared risk factor of smoking.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Emphysema','hard','mcq','The NRF2 transcription factor plays a protective role in emphysema. Which statement best describes its function?','["A. NRF2 promotes alveolar macrophage recruitment; its activation worsens emphysema by increasing proteolysis","B. NRF2 suppresses neutrophil elastase production; its deficiency directly causes panacinar emphysema","C. NRF2 upregulates genes protecting cells from oxidant damage; mice lacking NRF2 are more sensitive to tobacco smoke","D. NRF2 inhibits TGF-beta signaling; loss of NRF2 causes fibrosis rather than emphysema"]','C','NRF2 is a transcription factor that senses oxidants in alveolar epithelial cells and upregulates multiple genes protecting cells from oxidant damage. Mice lacking NRF2 are significantly more sensitive to tobacco smoke than normal mice. Genetic variants in NRF2, its regulators, and NRF2 target genes are all associated with smoking-related lung disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Chronic Bronchitis','easy','mcq','Chronic bronchitis is defined clinically as:','["A. Productive cough for at least 3 consecutive months in at least 2 consecutive years","B. FEV1/FVC ratio less than 0.7 with a positive bronchodilator response","C. Recurrent lower respiratory tract infections more than 4 times per year","D. Radiological evidence of hyperinflation with flattened diaphragms"]','D','Chronic bronchitis is a clinical diagnosis defined as a persistent productive cough for at least 3 consecutive months in at least 2 consecutive years in a patient in whom other causes of chronic cough have been excluded. It reflects mucus hypersecretion from bronchial gland hyperplasia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Chronic Bronchitis','easy','mcq','Which pathologic finding is the hallmark of chronic bronchitis on histology?','["A. Subepithelial eosinophilic infiltration with basement membrane thickening","B. Destruction of alveolar walls with airspace enlargement","C. Mucous gland hyperplasia in the bronchial wall with increased Reid index","D. Granulomatous inflammation with Langhans giant cells"]','A','The hallmark of chronic bronchitis is hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the mucus-secreting glands in the bronchi, reflected by an increased Reid index (ratio of mucous gland thickness to bronchial wall thickness). Normal Reid index is 0.4 or less; in chronic bronchitis it exceeds 0.4.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Chronic Bronchitis','medium','mcq','A 58-year-old obese male smoker presents with chronic productive cough, cyanosis, and peripheral edema. He has a PaO2 of 55 mmHg and PaCO2 of 55 mmHg. Which clinical phenotype does this patient represent?','["A. Pink puffer","B. Asthmatic bronchitis","C. Blue bloater","D. Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis"]','C','This patient exhibits the "blue bloater" phenotype of chronic bronchitis: cyanosis (from hypoxemia and hypercapnia), peripheral edema (from cor pulmonale/right heart failure), obesity, and chronic productive cough. These patients tend to hypoventilate and retain CO2, in contrast to "pink puffers" with emphysema who maintain oxygenation through hyperventilation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Chronic Bronchitis','medium','mcq','The Reid index is used to assess the severity of chronic bronchitis. Which measurement does it represent?','["A. Ratio of alveolar dead space to tidal volume","B. Ratio of airway lumen diameter to total bronchial diameter","C. Ratio of mucous gland thickness to total bronchial wall thickness","D. Ratio of FEV1 to FVC after bronchodilator administration"]','C','The Reid index measures the ratio of the thickness of the mucous gland layer to the total thickness of the bronchial wall (from epithelium to perichondrium). In normal individuals this ratio is 0.4 or less. In chronic bronchitis, gland hyperplasia increases this ratio above 0.4, correlating with the degree of mucus hypersecretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Chronic Bronchitis','medium','mcq','A biopsy from a patient with chronic bronchitis shows goblet cell hyperplasia extending into bronchioles. Which additional microscopic finding is most consistent with this diagnosis?','["A. Hypertrophy of submucosal mucous glands with inflammatory infiltrate of neutrophils and lymphocytes","B. Infiltration of bronchial walls with eosinophils and Curschmann spirals","C. Destruction of alveolar walls and loss of interalveolar septa","D. Noncaseating granulomas within the bronchial submucosa"]','A','In chronic bronchitis, microscopic findings include hypertrophy and hyperplasia of mucous glands in the bronchi (increased Reid index), goblet cell hyperplasia extending into bronchioles, inflammatory cell infiltrate (predominantly neutrophils and lymphocytes), and small airway disease with peribronchial fibrosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Chronic Bronchitis','medium','mcq','Which of the following is the most important risk factor for developing chronic bronchitis?','["A. Occupational exposure to asbestos","B. Recurrent viral pneumonias","C. Cigarette smoking and air pollutants","D. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"]','B','The major causes of chronic bronchitis are tobacco smoke and air pollutants. These irritants stimulate mucus hypersecretion by goblet cells and submucosal glands, as well as impairing mucociliary clearance, leading to the pathologic changes of chronic bronchitis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Chronic Bronchitis','medium','mcq','In a patient with COPD, which bacteria most commonly causes acute exacerbations?','["A. Staphylococcus aureus","B. Klebsiella pneumoniae","C. Haemophilus influenzae","D. Pseudomonas aeruginosa"]','B','Haemophilus influenzae is the most common bacterial cause of acute exacerbation of COPD, as stated in Robbins. Moraxella catarrhalis is the second most common. These bacteria colonize the lower airways and trigger inflammatory flares in the setting of chronic mucociliary dysfunction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Chronic Bronchitis','hard','mcq','Which small-airway changes in chronic bronchitis contribute most directly to irreversible airflow obstruction?','["A. Loss of cartilaginous support leading to bronchiectatic dilation","B. Subepithelial eosinophilic infiltration causing reversible bronchoconstriction","C. Alveolar wall destruction reducing elastic recoil","D. Smooth muscle hypertrophy, peribronchial fibrosis, and mucus plugging narrowing bronchiolar lumina"]','C','In chronic bronchitis, small-airway disease (chronic bronchiolitis) contributes to obstruction through goblet cell hyperplasia with mucus plugging, inflammatory infiltrates (neutrophils, macrophages, B and T cells), and bronchiolar wall thickening from smooth muscle hypertrophy and peribronchial fibrosis. These narrow the bronchiolar lumen and cause irreversible obstruction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Chronic Bronchitis','hard','mcq','A patient with long-standing chronic bronchitis develops cor pulmonale. What is the primary mechanism linking chronic bronchitis to right ventricular hypertrophy?','["A. Pulmonary fibrosis obliterating capillaries reduces cross-sectional area of the vascular bed","B. Recurrent pulmonary emboli from deep vein thromboses occlude pulmonary arteries","C. Chronic hypoxemia-induced pulmonary vasoconstriction leads to pulmonary hypertension and increased right ventricular afterload","D. Left ventricular failure due to coronary artery disease causes secondary right heart strain"]','B','In chronic bronchitis, mucus plugging and airway obstruction cause chronic hypoxemia. Hypoxia produces pulmonary vasoconstriction (hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction), which chronically elevates pulmonary vascular resistance, leading to pulmonary hypertension. The right ventricle hypertrophies to overcome increased afterload, eventually leading to cor pulmonale and right heart failure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Obstructive Lung Disease — Chronic Bronchitis','hard','mcq','Which finding would be most characteristic of chronic bronchitis compared to normal airways?','["A. Goblet cells confined to trachea and mainstem bronchi only","B. Replacement of goblet cells by ciliated columnar cells throughout the bronchial tree","C. Goblet cell hyperplasia extending into bronchioles where goblet cells are normally absent","D. Loss of goblet cells in the trachea with compensatory hyperplasia in alveoli"]','C','In normal airways, goblet cells and submucosal glands are present in the trachea and bronchi but not in bronchioles. In chronic bronchitis, goblet cell hyperplasia extends into the bronchioles (where they are normally absent), and submucosal mucous glands throughout the bronchial tree are hypertrophied, contributing to mucus hypersecretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Asthma','easy','mcq','Which immunoglobulin class is primarily responsible for triggering mast cell degranulation in atopic asthma?','["A. IgA","B. IgE","C. IgM","D. IgG"]','D','Atopic asthma is a classic example of IgE-mediated (type I) hypersensitivity. Allergens stimulate TH2 cells and B cells to produce IgE, which binds to Fc receptors on submucosal mast cells. Re-exposure to antigen cross-links IgE, triggering mast cell degranulation and release of bronchoconstrictor mediators.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Asthma','easy','mcq','Charcot-Leyden crystals found in the sputum of asthmatic patients are derived from which cell type?','["A. Neutrophils","B. Eosinophils","C. Goblet cells","D. Mast cells"]','D','Charcot-Leyden crystals are derived from eosinophil granule proteins (lysophospholipase). Their presence in sputum reflects significant eosinophilic airway inflammation, characteristic of allergic/atopic asthma. They appear as elongated, bipyramidal, needle-like crystals.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Asthma','medium','mcq','A 25-year-old woman presents with episodic wheezing and dyspnea. Sputum shows Curschmann spirals and Charcot-Leyden crystals. Which pathologic feature on biopsy confirms chronic asthmatic airway remodeling?','["A. Destruction of alveolar walls with panacinar emphysema","B. Goblet cell hyperplasia limited to trachea with normal bronchioles","C. Noncaseating granulomas with asteroid bodies","D. Thickened basement membrane, smooth muscle hypertrophy, and submucosal eosinophilic infiltration"]','B','Asthmatic airway remodeling includes: thickening of the basement membrane, smooth muscle hyperplasia and hypertrophy, angiogenesis, subepithelial fibrosis, mucous gland hypertrophy, and a dense inflammatory infiltrate rich in eosinophils, mast cells, and lymphocytes (TH2 phenotype). Curschmann spirals are mucus plugs cast from small bronchioles.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Asthma','medium','mcq','A patient with nasal polyps develops severe bronchospasm after taking ibuprofen for back pain. What is the mechanism of drug-induced asthma in this case?','["A. IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation triggered by ibuprofen as an allergen","B. Inhibition of cyclooxygenase decreasing prostaglandin E2 and promoting leukotriene production","C. Direct stimulation of H1 receptors causing bronchoconstriction","D. Activation of beta-adrenergic receptors increasing airway smooth muscle tone"]','C','Aspirin and NSAIDs cause asthma in sensitive individuals by inhibiting the cyclooxygenase (COX) pathway of arachidonic acid metabolism. This decreases prostaglandin E2 (which normally inhibits leukotriene synthesis) and shunts arachidonic acid toward the lipoxygenase pathway, generating leukotrienes B4, C4, D4, and E4 that cause bronchoconstriction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Asthma','medium','mcq','Which cytokine profile is most characteristic of atopic asthma, and which cytokine specifically activates eosinophils?','["A. TH1 profile (IFN-gamma, IL-2); eosinophils activated by IL-12","B. TH17 profile (IL-17, IL-22); eosinophils activated by IL-6","C. TH2 profile (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13); eosinophils activated by IL-5","D. Treg profile (IL-10, TGF-beta); eosinophils activated by IL-10"]','B','Atopic asthma is driven by a TH2 helper T cell response. Key TH2 cytokines include IL-4 (stimulates IgE production), IL-5 (activates and recruits eosinophils), and IL-13 (promotes mucus secretion and IgE production). This TH2/eosinophilic phenotype responds well to corticosteroids.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Asthma','medium','mcq','Status asthmaticus is defined as:','["A. Asthma associated with peripheral blood eosinophilia greater than 1500 cells per microliter","B. Asthma with documented FEV1/FVC ratio less than 0.5 on spirometry","C. A state of unremitting severe asthma attacks that may prove fatal","D. Nocturnal asthma episodes occurring more than 4 nights per week"]','A','Status asthmaticus (now called acute severe asthma) is defined as a state of unremitting attacks of asthma that may prove fatal. These patients typically have a long history of asthma. The condition represents a medical emergency due to progressive respiratory failure if untreated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Asthma','medium','mcq','The "hygiene hypothesis" proposes that increased asthma incidence in industrialized societies is related to which mechanism?','["A. Limited early childhood microbial exposure leading to failure to suppress TH2 responses","B. Increased indoor allergen concentrations in airtight buildings","C. Higher levels of industrial air pollutants directly damaging bronchial epithelium","D. Genetic drift causing increased prevalence of atopic alleles in urban populations"]','B','The hygiene hypothesis proposes that city life limits early childhood exposure to microbial antigens, and such exposure seems to protect children from asthma and atopy. Microbial exposure during early development (including in utero) reduces later incidence of allergic diseases, possibly by promoting TH1 maturation and suppressing excessive TH2 responses.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Asthma','hard','mcq','In fatal status asthmaticus, autopsy of the lungs most characteristically shows:','["A. Lungs overinflated with viscid mucus plugs occluding bronchi, eosinophilic infiltrates, and hypertrophied bronchial smooth muscle","B. Extensive emphysematous destruction of alveolar walls with bullae formation","C. Diffuse hyaline membrane formation with intra-alveolar fibrin deposits","D. Bilateral lower lobe consolidation with neutrophilic exudate filling alveoli"]','B','In fatal asthma, the lungs are overinflated due to air trapping. Classic findings include viscid mucus plugs filling bronchi (Curschmann spirals), marked eosinophilic bronchial infiltration, hypertrophy of bronchial smooth muscle, thickened basement membranes, and mucous gland hypertrophy — all reflecting chronic airway remodeling superimposed on acute exacerbation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Asthma','hard','mcq','A genetic study identifies a susceptibility locus for asthma on chromosome 5q containing cytokine genes. Which gene polymorphism in this cluster has the strongest and most consistent association with asthma?','["A. IL-13 gene polymorphism","B. IL-4 gene polymorphism","C. IL-9 gene polymorphism","D. IL-3 gene polymorphism"]','B','The chromosome 5q locus contains a cluster of cytokine genes including IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9, IL-13, and the IL-4 receptor. Among these, polymorphisms in the IL-13 gene have the strongest and most consistent associations with asthma or allergic disease, according to Robbins.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Asthma','hard','mcq','An asthmatic patient with occupational exposure to toluene diisocyanate develops attacks specifically after work. Which mechanism best explains occupational asthma?','["A. Multiple possible mechanisms including type I IgE reactions, direct liberation of bronchoconstrictor substances, or hypersensitivity responses of unknown origin","B. IgE-mediated immediate hypersensitivity to toluene acting as a hapten — the only mechanism","C. Non-specific irritant effect of all industrial chemicals on bronchial mucosa without sensitization","D. Psychosomatic bronchoconstriction triggered by the stress of the work environment"]','A','Occupational asthma can be triggered by fumes, organic and chemical dusts, gases, or other chemicals. The underlying mechanisms vary — including type I IgE reactions, direct liberation of bronchoconstrictor substances, and hypersensitivity responses of unknown origin. Only minute quantities are required after repeated exposure leads to sensitization.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Bronchiectasis','easy','mcq','Bronchiectasis is characterized by which pathologic change?','["A. Reversible narrowing of bronchi due to smooth muscle spasm","B. Goblet cell metaplasia in respiratory bronchioles causing mucus hypersecretion","C. Permanent dilation of bronchi and bronchioles due to destruction of smooth muscle and elastic tissue","D. Transient mucus plugging of bronchioles causing distal atelectasis"]','D','Bronchiectasis is defined as a disorder in which destruction of smooth muscle and elastic tissue by chronic necrotizing infections leads to permanent dilation of bronchi and bronchioles. The dilation is irreversible (unlike asthma), and results from the combined effects of obstruction and infection.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Bronchiectasis','easy','mcq','Which hereditary condition is most commonly associated with severe widespread bronchiectasis?','["A. Marfan syndrome","B. Cystic fibrosis","C. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","D. Kartagener syndrome"]','C','Cystic fibrosis is the most common hereditary condition causing severe, widespread bronchiectasis. The CFTR defect leads to thick viscous secretions impairing mucociliary clearance, recurrent bacterial infections (especially Pseudomonas aeruginosa), and progressive airway wall destruction with bronchiectatic dilation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Bronchiectasis','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old man with recurrent sinopulmonary infections, infertility, and situs inversus is found to have bronchiectasis. What is the underlying pathogenetic mechanism?','["A. Defective mucociliary clearance due to absent or dysfunctional dynein arms in cilia","B. IgA deficiency causing impaired mucosal immunity","C. Bronchial obstruction by a mucus-secreting carcinoid tumor","D. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency causing protease-antiprotease imbalance in airways"]','A','This is Kartagener syndrome (a variant of primary ciliary dyskinesia): situs inversus + bronchiectasis + sinusitis. Primary ciliary dyskinesia is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in ciliary motor proteins (e.g., dynein). Ciliary dysfunction leads to retention of secretions, recurrent infections, and bronchiectasis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Bronchiectasis','medium','mcq','A patient with bronchiectasis presents with paroxysms of purulent foul-smelling sputum production upon arising in the morning. What is the pathophysiologic explanation for this timing?','["A. Nocturnal surfactant depletion causes airway collapse and secretion accumulation","B. Peak diurnal airway inflammation occurs in the morning","C. Positional change from lying to standing drains pooled pus and secretions into major bronchi","D. Morning cortisol surge triggers mast cell degranulation worsening airway inflammation"]','A','In bronchiectasis, pus and secretions pool in the dilated bronchi during sleep. Upon arising in the morning, the change in position drains these collections from the dependent dilated bronchi into the larger airways, triggering paroxysms of productive cough. This is the classic presentation of bronchiectasis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Bronchiectasis','medium','mcq','Which two fundamental conditions must typically be present simultaneously for full-fledged bronchiectasis to develop?','["A. Viral infection and immunodeficiency","B. Obstruction and infection","C. Smoking and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","D. Connective tissue disease and pulmonary fibrosis"]','C','According to Robbins, obstruction and infection are the two major conditions that together promote the development of bronchiectasis. Obstruction impairs normal clearing mechanisms, causing pooling of secretions distal to the obstruction, which then invites secondary infection. Conversely, severe infections lead to inflammation, necrosis, fibrosis, and progressive airway dilation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Bronchiectasis','medium','mcq','Which of the following is NOT a recognized cause of bronchiectasis?','["A. Rheumatoid arthritis","B. Centriacinar emphysema from cigarette smoking","C. Immotile cilia syndrome","D. Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis"]','B','Centriacinar emphysema is not a cause of bronchiectasis. Recognized causes include: hereditary conditions (cystic fibrosis, primary ciliary dyskinesia, immunodeficiency), severe/recurrent infections, bronchial obstruction, and other conditions such as rheumatoid arthritis, SLE, inflammatory bowel disease, COPD, and post-transplantation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Bronchiectasis','medium','mcq','In cystic fibrosis-related bronchiectasis, which bacterial organism most commonly colonizes the airways chronically?','["A. Streptococcus pneumoniae","B. Haemophilus influenzae","C. Pseudomonas aeruginosa (mucoid form)","D. Staphylococcus epidermidis"]','C','In cystic fibrosis, the chronically damaged airways become persistently colonized with mucoid Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which forms biofilms resistant to antibiotics and host defenses. This chronic infection drives further airway destruction and bronchiectasis progression. Robbins lists mucoid and nonmucoid Pseudomonas aeruginosa among the typical CF pathogens.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Bronchiectasis','hard','mcq','Before modern treatment, which was the most important complication of bronchiectasis cited by Robbins as now less frequent due to better antibiotics and physical therapy?','["A. Secondary AA amyloidosis causing renal failure","B. Spontaneous pneumothorax","C. Mycobacterial superinfection","D. Cor pulmonale and brain abscesses"]','D','Before modern antibiotics and physical therapy, major complications of bronchiectasis included cor pulmonale, brain abscesses (from hematogenous spread), and amyloidosis. Robbins specifically states these complications are now less frequent due to better antibiotics and physical therapy, and that life expectancy has almost doubled.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Bronchiectasis','hard','mcq','Primary ciliary dyskinesia has a frequency of 1 in 15,000 to 40,000 births. Approximately what proportion of patients have Kartagener syndrome (situs inversus)?','["A. 10%","B. 50%","C. 25%","D. 75%"]','C','According to Robbins, approximately half of patients with primary ciliary dyskinesia have Kartagener syndrome (situs inversus or partial lateralizing abnormality plus bronchiectasis and sinusitis). Ciliary function is required during development for normal rotation of organs in the chest and abdomen; in its absence, organ location becomes a matter of chance, resulting in situs inversus in approximately 50%.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Bronchiectasis','hard','mcq','In cystic fibrosis-associated bronchiectasis, what process leads to progressive obliteration of smaller bronchioles while larger bronchi become markedly dilated?','["A. Goblet cell metaplasia filling bronchiolar lumina with mucus","B. Smooth muscle hypertrophy causing irreversible bronchospasm in small airways","C. Granulomatous inflammation in bronchioles secondary to Pseudomonas infection","D. Bronchiolitis obliterans — fibrosis progressively obliterating smaller bronchioles"]','C','In cystic fibrosis-associated bronchiectasis, larger bronchi become markedly dilated due to destruction of supporting smooth muscle and elastic tissue, while smaller bronchioles are progressively obliterated by fibrosis (bronchiolitis obliterans). This dual process reflects the combined effects of chronic infection, inflammation, and scarring.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Restrictive Lung Disease — Interstitial Lung Diseases','easy','mcq','Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) is characterized by which histologic pattern?','["A. Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia with uniform inflammation","B. Diffuse alveolar damage with hyaline membranes","C. Usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) with patchy temporally heterogeneous fibrosis","D. Organizing pneumonia with Masson bodies in alveolar ducts"]','A','IPF is characterized by the histologic pattern of usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP). The UIP pattern features patchy interstitial fibrosis that varies in intensity and age (temporal heterogeneity), with the earliest lesions showing fibroblastic foci and older lesions showing dense collagen. This temporal heterogeneity distinguishes UIP from other ILD patterns.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Restrictive Lung Disease — Interstitial Lung Diseases','easy','mcq','End-stage interstitial lung disease results in a characteristic gross pathologic appearance. What is this called?','["A. Panacinar emphysema","B. Consolidation","C. Bullous emphysema","D. Honeycomb lung"]','C','Advanced interstitial lung diseases, regardless of etiology, all result in scarring and gross destruction of the lung parenchyma, referred to as "honeycomb lung" (end-stage lung). This appears as a coarse fibrotic pattern with irregular cystic spaces lined by bronchiolar-type epithelium, visible grossly and on HRCT as peripheral, subpleural honeycombing.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Restrictive Lung Disease — Interstitial Lung Diseases','medium','mcq','A 62-year-old male ex-smoker presents with progressive dyspnea, dry cough, finger clubbing, and bilateral basal inspiratory crackles. HRCT shows bilateral subpleural reticular opacities with honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis. What is the median survival after diagnosis of IPF?','["A. 10 years","B. 3 years","C. 6 months","D. 5 years"]','B','IPF has a poor prognosis with a median survival of approximately 3 years after diagnosis. Patients present in the 55-75 age group with gradual dyspnea on exertion and dry cough. The course is unpredictable — some have gradual deterioration, others have acute exacerbations. Lung transplantation is the only definitive therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Restrictive Lung Disease — Interstitial Lung Diseases','medium','mcq','Which genetic mutations are associated with familial forms of IPF and indicate a role for cellular senescence?','["A. CFTR mutations causing abnormal chloride transport in alveolar epithelium","B. TERT and TERC mutations causing telomere shortening and alveolar epithelial cell senescence","C. SERPINA1 mutations causing protease-antiprotease imbalance","D. MUC5B loss-of-function mutations reducing mucus protection"]','C','Familial IPF is associated with germline loss-of-function mutations in TERT and TERC genes, which encode components of telomerase. Telomerase maintains telomere length; its loss causes accelerated telomere shortening and alveolar epithelial cell senescence (aging), which appears to be the initiating event in IPF pathogenesis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Restrictive Lung Disease — Interstitial Lung Diseases','medium','mcq','Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) differs from IPF/UIP in which important prognostic way?','["A. NSIP has worse prognosis because it is more resistant to corticosteroids","B. NSIP has much better prognosis than UIP and may be idiopathic or associated with connective tissue disease","C. NSIP is more likely to progress to lung carcinoma than UIP","D. NSIP exclusively affects males over 60 years of age unlike UIP"]','D','Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) is important to recognize because patients have a much better prognosis than those with usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP/IPF). NSIP may be idiopathic or associated with connective tissue disease. Patients are more likely to be female nonsmokers in their sixth decade of life.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Restrictive Lung Disease — Interstitial Lung Diseases','medium','mcq','In cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (COP), what are the characteristic intra-alveolar structures seen on biopsy?','["A. Hyaline membranes lining alveolar walls","B. Noncaseating granulomas with multinucleated giant cells","C. Diffuse lymphoid hyperplasia replacing alveolar walls","D. Polypoid plugs of loose organizing connective tissue (Masson bodies) within alveolar ducts and alveoli"]','C','Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (formerly BOOP) is characterized by the presence of polypoid plugs of loose organizing connective tissue (Masson bodies) within alveolar ducts, alveoli, and often bronchioles. The connective tissue is all of the same age (temporally homogeneous), and the underlying lung architecture is normal. Most patients respond to oral steroids.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Restrictive Lung Disease — Interstitial Lung Diseases','medium','mcq','Pulmonary function testing in restrictive lung disease typically shows which pattern?','["A. Reduced FEV1 with normal FVC and low FEV1/FVC ratio","B. Increased compliance with decreased diffusion capacity","C. Proportionate reduction in both FVC and FEV1 with normal FEV1/FVC ratio and reduced total lung capacity","D. Markedly increased residual volume with air trapping"]','D','Restrictive lung diseases show proportionate decreases in both total lung capacity and FEV1, maintaining a normal or even elevated FEV1/FVC ratio (in contrast to obstructive disease where the ratio falls below 0.7). Classic functional abnormalities include reductions in diffusion capacity, lung volumes, and lung compliance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Restrictive Lung Disease — Interstitial Lung Diseases','hard','mcq','In IPF, the cobblestoned pleural surface seen grossly is caused by which mechanism?','["A. Pleural mesothelioma invading the visceral pleura","B. Pleural plaques from asbestos exposure creating irregular nodular deposits","C. Subpleural emphysematous bullae deforming the pleural surface","D. Retraction of scars along interlobular septa causing the pleural surface to dimple and pucker"]','D','Grossly, in IPF the pleural surfaces of the lung are cobblestoned as a result of the retraction of scars along the interlobular septa. The cut surface shows firm, rubbery white areas of fibrosis, occurring preferentially in the lower lobes, subpleural regions, and along interlobular septa.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Restrictive Lung Disease — Interstitial Lung Diseases','hard','mcq','Approximately what proportion of IPF cases are associated with a common MUC5B genetic variant that increases mucin secretion?','["A. One-third","B. 90%","C. Two-thirds","D. 5%"]','D','According to Robbins, roughly one-third of IPF cases are associated with a common genetic variant that greatly increases the secretion of MUC5B, a mucin. This mucin may make alveolar epithelial cells susceptible to injury or may exaggerate downstream events leading to fibrosis.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Restrictive Lung Disease — Interstitial Lung Diseases','hard','mcq','A patient with systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) develops ILD. Which histologic ILD pattern is most commonly seen in scleroderma?','["A. Usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern","B. Organizing pneumonia (OP) pattern","C. Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) pattern","D. Lymphoid interstitial pneumonia (LIP) pattern"]','C','In scleroderma (progressive systemic sclerosis), pulmonary involvement most commonly shows a nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) pattern, which is more common than the UIP pattern. This is in contrast to IPF where UIP is the defining pattern. Rheumatoid arthritis may show UIP, NSIP, or other patterns.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pneumoconioses & Occupational Lung Disease','easy','mcq','Which pneumoconiosis is associated with an increased susceptibility to tuberculosis?','["A. Asbestosis","B. Silicosis","C. Coal workers'' pneumoconiosis","D. Berylliosis"]','A','Silicosis is associated with increased susceptibility to tuberculosis. This may be because crystalline silica inhibits macrophage killing of mycobacteria. In contrast, coal workers'''' pneumoconiosis does not convincingly increase susceptibility to tuberculosis, according to Robbins.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pneumoconioses & Occupational Lung Disease','easy','mcq','Which form of asbestos fiber is most pathogenic and most strongly associated with mesothelioma?','["A. Amphibole forms (crocidolite, amosite)","B. Chrysotile (serpentine form)","C. Anthophyllite","D. Tremolite"]','B','Amphibole asbestos fibers (including crocidolite and amosite) are more pathogenic than chrysotile (serpentine form), particularly with respect to mesothelioma induction. Chrysotile fibers are flexible and curled, becoming impacted in upper airways. Amphiboles are straight and stiff, penetrating deeper into the lungs to reach the interstitium and pleura.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pneumoconioses & Occupational Lung Disease','medium','mcq','A shipyard worker develops progressive dyspnea and bilateral lower lobe crackles 25 years after exposure. HRCT shows irregular linear densities in lower lobes with a honeycomb pattern. Biopsy reveals ferruginous bodies. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Silicosis","B. Asbestosis","C. Coal workers'' pneumoconiosis","D. Berylliosis"]','B','This presentation is classic for asbestosis: long latency (rarely appearing fewer than 10 years after first exposure, more common after 20-30 years), dyspnea, lower lobe irregular linear densities progressing to honeycomb pattern, and ferruginous bodies (asbestos fibers coated with iron-protein) on biopsy. Shipyard workers are a classic at-risk group.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pneumoconioses & Occupational Lung Disease','medium','mcq','What is the most dangerous particle size for causing pneumoconiosis, and why?','["A. 1-5 micrometers, because they reach terminal airways and alveoli and settle in their linings","B. Greater than 10 micrometers, because larger particles carry more mineral dust","C. Less than 0.5 micrometers, because they are too small for macrophages to phagocytose","D. 5-10 micrometers, because they deposit at the mucociliary junction"]','B','The most dangerous particles are 1-5 micrometers in diameter. Larger particles (>10 µm) are trapped in the nose and upper airways. Particles 1-5 µm reach terminal small airways and alveoli, settling in their linings and evading mucociliary clearance. Particles less than 0.5 µm may behave like gases and be exhaled without deposition.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pneumoconioses & Occupational Lung Disease','medium','mcq','Silicosis is the most prevalent chronic occupational disease in the world. Which mechanism explains its fibrogenic effect?','["A. Silica directly cross-links collagen fibers causing irreversible scarring","B. Phagocytosed silica crystals activate the inflammasome leading to IL-1 and IL-18 release, driving fibrogenic macrophage activation","C. Silica inhibits surfactant production causing alveolar collapse and secondary fibrosis","D. Silica acts as a carcinogen causing p53 mutations in alveolar type II cells"]','A','After inhalation, silica particles are phagocytosed by macrophages. The phagocytosed crystalline silica activates the inflammasome, leading to release of inflammatory mediators, particularly IL-1 and IL-18. These cytokines drive macrophage activation and fibrogenesis, resulting in the characteristic silicotic nodules with concentric whorls of hyalinized collagen.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pneumoconioses & Occupational Lung Disease','medium','mcq','Which of the following occupational lung diseases produces noncaseating granulomas histologically, resembling sarcoidosis?','["A. Berylliosis","B. Silicosis","C. Coal workers'' pneumoconiosis","D. Asbestosis"]','C','Berylliosis (chronic beryllium disease) produces noncaseating granulomas histologically indistinguishable from those of sarcoidosis. It is an immune-mediated granulomatous reaction to beryllium, seen in workers in aerospace, electronics, and nuclear industries. The diagnosis requires a history of beryllium exposure and positive beryllium lymphocyte proliferation test (BeLPT).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pneumoconioses & Occupational Lung Disease','medium','mcq','Pleural plaques in asbestosis are typically:','["A. Symptomatic and associated with severe dyspnea","B. Composed of calcium oxalate crystals deposited in visceral pleura","C. Associated with malignant transformation to mesothelioma in 50% of cases","D. Asymptomatic, detected radiographically as circumscribed densities on parietal pleura"]','D','Pleural plaques in asbestosis are usually asymptomatic and are detected on radiographs as circumscribed densities on the parietal pleura. They represent localized areas of parietal pleural fibrosis. While they indicate asbestos exposure, they do not necessarily predict the development of malignant mesothelioma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pneumoconioses & Occupational Lung Disease','hard','mcq','A coal miner develops progressive massive fibrosis (PMF). Which statement accurately distinguishes PMF from simple coal workers'''' pneumoconiosis (CWP)?','["A. PMF is reversible with cessation of coal dust exposure, whereas simple CWP is permanent","B. PMF affects only the upper lobes, whereas simple CWP is diffuse","C. PMF is exclusively caused by coal dust, whereas simple CWP may also be caused by silica contamination","D. PMF causes significant pulmonary dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension, and cor pulmonale, and may progress even after dust exposure ceases"]','B','Progressive massive fibrosis (complicated CWP) develops in fewer than 10% of coal workers and causes significant pulmonary dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension, and cor pulmonale. Crucially, once PMF develops, it may continue to worsen even if further dust exposure is prevented, distinguishing it from simple CWP which rarely compromises lung function.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pneumoconioses & Occupational Lung Disease','hard','mcq','Which statement best explains why asbestos can cause both pulmonary fibrosis and mesothelioma, unlike most other pneumoconiosis-causing dusts?','["A. Asbestos has a much smaller particle size than silica, allowing deeper lung penetration","B. Asbestos can act as a tumor initiator and promoter through reactive free radical generation, in addition to being fibrogenic","C. Asbestos selectively depletes T lymphocytes, impairing tumor surveillance","D. Asbestos mimics the chemical structure of DNA bases, directly causing oncogenic mutations"]','B','Unlike other inorganic dusts, asbestos can act as both a tumor initiator and promoter. Some of its oncogenic effects are mediated by reactive free radicals generated by asbestos fibers, which can cause oxidative DNA damage. Combined with its physical properties (persistent long thin fibers reaching the pleura), this explains why asbestos causes both fibrosis and mesothelioma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pneumoconioses & Occupational Lung Disease','hard','mcq','A worker who sandblasts denim develops acute silicosis within months. What pathological hallmark distinguishes acute silicosis from chronic silicosis?','["A. Accumulation of abundant lipoproteinaceous material within alveoli, identical to pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","B. Cavitation of silicotic nodules due to superimposed tuberculosis","C. Concentric hyalinized collagenous silicotic nodules in upper lobes","D. Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy with eggshell calcification"]','C','Acute silicosis follows heavy exposure over months to a few years (as in denim sandblasting) and is characterized by accumulation of abundant lipoproteinaceous material within alveoli — identical morphologically to pulmonary alveolar proteinosis. This differs from chronic silicosis, which shows whorled silicotic nodules with concentric rings of hyalinized collagen in upper lung zones.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Infections','easy','mcq','Lobar pneumonia typically results in consolidation of an entire lobe. Which organism most commonly causes classic lobar pneumonia?','["A. Staphylococcus aureus","B. Streptococcus pneumoniae","C. Klebsiella pneumoniae","D. Haemophilus influenzae"]','C','Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcus) is the most common cause of lobar pneumonia in adults. The classic sequence of lobar pneumonia has four stages: congestion, red hepatization, grey hepatization, and resolution. The pores of Kohn allow spread of bacteria between alveoli within the lobe.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Infections','easy','mcq','In which location does aspirated foreign material tend to localize, and why?','["A. Right lower lobe, because the right mainstem bronchus is more vertical and directly in line with the trachea","B. Left lower lobe, because the left bronchus is narrower","C. Left upper lobe, because the left bronchus bifurcates at a greater angle","D. Right middle lobe, because it is the smallest lobe and most susceptible to collapse"]','B','The right mainstem bronchus is more vertical and directly in line with the trachea. Consequently, aspirated foreign materials (vomitus, blood, foreign bodies) tend to enter the right lung more often than the left. This is why aspiration pneumonia and lung abscesses from aspiration tend to occur in the right lower lobe.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Infections','medium','mcq','A 68-year-old immunocompromised patient on corticosteroids develops fever, dyspnea, and hypoxia. Bronchoalveolar lavage with Gomori methenamine silver (GMS) stain reveals cup-shaped cysts. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Invasive aspergillosis","B. Cytomegalovirus pneumonia","C. Cryptococcal pneumonia","D. Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP)"]','D','Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP) is a fungal infection seen in immunocompromised patients (HIV/AIDS, organ transplant, high-dose steroids). GMS stain reveals characteristic cup-shaped or helmet-shaped cysts (4-6 µm). BAL shows foamy eosinophilic exudate filling alveoli. PCP is the most common opportunistic pulmonary infection in HIV-positive patients with low CD4 counts.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Infections','medium','mcq','Which type of pneumonia involves patchy consolidation centered on bronchi and bronchioles, commonly seen in debilitated or hospitalized patients?','["A. Bronchopneumonia (lobular pneumonia)","B. Lobar pneumonia","C. Interstitial pneumonia","D. Organizing pneumonia"]','A','Bronchopneumonia (lobular pneumonia) involves patchy, multifocal areas of consolidation centered on bronchi and bronchioles, spreading to adjacent alveoli. It is more common in the very young, elderly, and debilitated patients, and is typically caused by Staphylococcus, Klebsiella, or gram-negative organisms. In contrast, lobar pneumonia affects an entire lobe uniformly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Infections','medium','mcq','A patient with alcohol abuse presents with an upper lobe cavitary lesion, night sweats, and weight loss. Biopsy shows caseating granulomas with Langhans giant cells. What is the characteristic primary complex in tuberculosis?','["A. Firm white nodules resembling mesothelioma deposits","B. Multiple hemorrhagic infarcts in the periphery of the lung","C. Ghon complex — a parenchymal focus with caseating necrosis and ipsilateral hilar lymph node enlargement","D. Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy with noncaseating granulomas"]','C','Primary tuberculosis produces the Ghon complex: a peripheral parenchymal focus of caseous necrosis (Ghon focus) combined with ipsilateral hilar lymph node caseating granulomas. Secondary (reactivation) TB produces upper lobe cavitary lesions. Caseating granulomas with Langhans giant cells, central necrosis, and acid-fast bacilli are the histologic hallmarks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Infections','medium','mcq','Klebsiella pneumoniae pneumonia has a characteristic feature that distinguishes it from other community-acquired bacterial pneumonias. What is this feature?','["A. Produces thick, mucoid, hemorrhagic (currant-jelly) sputum and is associated with alcoholism","B. Characteristically causes bullous myringitis and cold agglutinins","C. Exclusively produces lobar consolidation of the right middle lobe","D. Predominantly affects immunocompetent children under 5 years old"]','D','Klebsiella pneumoniae characteristically produces thick, gelatinous, blood-tinged (currant-jelly) sputum due to its abundant polysaccharide capsule. It predominantly affects alcoholics, diabetics, and the debilitated elderly. It can cause rapid lung destruction with abscess formation and is a common cause of hospital-acquired pneumonia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Infections','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old HIV-positive patient with CD4 count of 50 cells per microliter develops progressive dyspnea and bilateral interstitial infiltrates. Which pulmonary infection is most characteristic of advanced HIV/AIDS?','["A. Streptococcus pneumoniae lobar pneumonia","B. Mycoplasma pneumoniae atypical pneumonia","C. Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","D. Legionella pneumophila Legionnaires'' disease"]','B','Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP) is the most common serious opportunistic pulmonary infection in HIV/AIDS. It occurs when CD4 count falls below 200 cells/µL and presents with progressive dyspnea, bilateral interstitial infiltrates (bilateral ground-glass opacities on CT), and hypoxia. It is listed in Robbins under pulmonary disease in HIV infection.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Infections','hard','mcq','The alveolar walls are perforated by pores of Kohn. What is the clinical significance of these pores in the context of pneumonia?','["A. They allow drainage of exudate from alveoli into pulmonary capillaries, reducing consolidation","B. They permit passage of bacteria and exudate between adjacent alveoli, facilitating spread of infection within the lobe","C. They serve as sites of surfactant secretion by type II pneumocytes during infection","D. They allow macrophages to migrate between alveoli to coordinate the immune response"]','D','The pores of Kohn are small holes in alveolar walls that normally allow collateral ventilation. In pneumonia, these pores permit the passage of bacteria and exudate between adjacent alveoli, facilitating the spread of infection throughout the lobe. This mechanism explains the lobar pattern of consolidation seen in pneumococcal pneumonia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Infections','hard','mcq','A lung abscess develops in the posterior segment of the right upper lobe. Which clinical scenario most likely caused this location?','["A. Direct extension from a Klebsiella lobar pneumonia in the right lower lobe","B. Hematogenous spread of infection from a distant septic source","C. Post-obstructive infection distal to a centrally located squamous cell carcinoma","D. Aspiration while in the supine position"]','C','The most common cause of lung abscess in adults is aspiration. When aspiration occurs while supine, material preferentially enters the posterior segments of the upper lobes or superior segments of the lower lobes due to gravity. The right side is more commonly affected because the right mainstem bronchus is more vertical. Hematogenous abscesses are multiple and bilateral.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Infections','hard','mcq','Legionella pneumophila causes Legionnaires'''' disease. Which of the following best characterizes its epidemiology and pathogenesis?','["A. It is transmitted person-to-person via respiratory droplets and causes outbreaks in schools","B. It is a strict intracellular parasite requiring specific tick vectors for transmission","C. It is a Gram-negative organism living in water systems and cooling towers, causing pneumonia particularly in smokers, elderly, and immunocompromised individuals","D. It produces a polysaccharide capsule enabling it to evade complement-mediated killing"]','B','Legionella pneumophila is a Gram-negative bacterium that lives in aquatic environments and becomes an infection risk through water aerosols from cooling towers, water systems, and humidifiers. It causes Legionnaires'''' disease particularly in older males, smokers, and immunocompromised individuals. It is an intracellular pathogen that replicates within alveolar macrophages.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Lung Carcinoma','easy','mcq','Which lung carcinoma is most strongly associated with cigarette smoking, arises centrally, and may cause hypercalcemia from PTHrP secretion?','["A. Adenocarcinoma","B. Small cell carcinoma","C. Large cell carcinoma","D. Squamous cell carcinoma"]','C','Squamous cell carcinoma is most strongly linked to cigarette smoking and arises centrally near the bronchi. It can produce PTHrP (parathyroid hormone-related protein), causing hypercalcemia — a paraneoplastic syndrome. It characteristically shows squamous differentiation (keratin pearls, intercellular bridges) and tends to cavitate.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Lung Carcinoma','easy','mcq','Which lung cancer subtype is most common in non-smokers and women, and arises peripherally?','["A. Adenocarcinoma","B. Small cell carcinoma","C. Large cell carcinoma","D. Squamous cell carcinoma"]','D','Adenocarcinoma is the most common lung cancer in non-smokers, women, and younger patients. It arises peripherally, often from bronchioloalveolar cells, and is the most common subtype overall in many Western countries. It frequently shows glandular differentiation, mucin production, and lepidic growth pattern. Driver mutations (EGFR, ALK, ROS1) are most common in adenocarcinoma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Lung Carcinoma','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old male smoker presents with a central hilar mass, superior vena cava syndrome, and elevated ACTH levels causing Cushing syndrome. Biopsy shows small cells with scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding, and neuroendocrine granules. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Squamous cell carcinoma producing PTHrP","B. Large cell carcinoma with beta-hCG production","C. Adenocarcinoma with lepidic growth pattern","D. Small cell carcinoma (SCLC) ectopically producing ACTH"]','B','Small cell carcinoma (SCLC) arises centrally, is strongly linked to smoking, and is the classic cause of paraneoplastic syndromes due to its neuroendocrine origin. ACTH production causes Cushing syndrome; ADH secretion causes SIADH; Lambert-Eaton syndrome causes proximal muscle weakness. SCLC shows nuclear molding, high mitotic rate, and crush artifact on biopsy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Lung Carcinoma','medium','mcq','Pancoast (superior sulcus) tumor causes a characteristic clinical syndrome. Which of the following is NOT part of Pancoast syndrome?','["A. Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) from sympathetic chain involvement","B. Superior vena cava syndrome from compression of the SVC","C. Rib and vertebral body destruction","D. Shoulder and arm pain from brachial plexus involvement"]','A','Pancoast syndrome results from lung carcinoma at the apex of the lung invading the brachial plexus (causing shoulder/arm pain), cervical sympathetic chain (causing Horner syndrome), and causing rib and vertebral destruction. Superior vena cava syndrome is caused by central tumors (particularly SCLC) compressing the SVC in the mediastinum, not apical tumors.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Lung Carcinoma','medium','mcq','Which paraneoplastic syndrome is most specifically associated with small cell lung carcinoma (SCLC), involving autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels?','["A. Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy","B. Hypercalcemia","C. Cushing syndrome","D. Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome"]','A','Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome is a paraneoplastic syndrome most specifically associated with SCLC. Autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels (P/Q-type VGCC) impair acetylcholine release, causing proximal muscle weakness that paradoxically improves with repeated stimulation (unlike myasthenia gravis). SCLC, being neuroendocrine in origin, expresses these neural antigens.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Lung Carcinoma','medium','mcq','Which molecular target is most commonly mutated in lung adenocarcinoma in non-smoking East Asian women, making it amenable to targeted therapy with tyrosine kinase inhibitors?','["A. KRAS","B. TP53","C. EGFR","D. RB1"]','A','EGFR (epidermal growth factor receptor) mutations are the most common targetable driver mutations in lung adenocarcinoma, especially in non-smoking East Asian women (up to 50% of cases). Exon 19 deletions and exon 21 L858R point mutations are sensitive to EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors (erlotinib, gefitinib, osimertinib).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Lung Carcinoma','medium','mcq','Large cell carcinoma of the lung is characterized by which features?','["A. Large polygonal cells with abundant cytoplasm, lacking squamous or glandular differentiation — a diagnosis of exclusion","B. Squamous differentiation with keratin pearls and intercellular bridges","C. Lepidic growth pattern along alveolar walls without stromal invasion","D. Small cells with nuclear molding, high N/C ratio, and neuroendocrine differentiation"]','D','Large cell carcinoma is a diagnosis of exclusion — it consists of large undifferentiated polygonal cells with abundant cytoplasm and prominent nucleoli that lack the features of squamous, glandular, or neuroendocrine differentiation by light microscopy and immunohistochemistry. It typically arises peripherally and has a poor prognosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Lung Carcinoma','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old never-smoker develops hyponatremia (Na+ 118 mEq/L) with no peripheral edema, normal thyroid and adrenal function, and inappropriately concentrated urine. Chest CT shows a central hilar mass. Which lung cancer most likely causes this paraneoplastic syndrome?','["A. Large cell carcinoma secreting beta-hCG","B. Adenocarcinoma secreting PTHrP","C. Squamous cell carcinoma secreting ACTH","D. Small cell carcinoma secreting ADH (SIADH)"]','A','SIADH (syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion) causing hyponatremia is most commonly caused by small cell lung carcinoma (SCLC). SCLC cells, being neuroendocrine in origin, ectopically secrete ADH (vasopressin), causing water retention, dilutional hyponatremia, and inappropriately concentrated urine. The absence of edema and normal renal/adrenal/thyroid function distinguish SIADH from other causes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Lung Carcinoma','hard','mcq','Which of the following statements about SCLC staging and prognosis is correct?','["A. SCLC is staged using the TNM system and surgery is the primary curative treatment","B. SCLC is staged as limited or extensive disease; extensive disease has a 5-year survival under 5%","C. Stage I SCLC has a 5-year survival of over 90% with chemotherapy alone","D. All lung carcinomas have a similar 5-year survival of 15-20% regardless of histologic type"]','D','SCLC is staged as limited disease (confined to one hemithorax) or extensive disease (beyond one hemithorax). By the time of diagnosis, approximately 70% of SCLC is extensive. SCLC is not surgically resectable; treatment is chemotherapy plus or minus radiation. Extensive SCLC has a very poor prognosis, with 5-year survival under 5%.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Lung Carcinoma','hard','mcq','A 45-year-old never-smoker develops a peripheral lung mass with ground-glass opacity on CT. Biopsy shows tumor cells growing along alveolar walls without stromal, vascular, or pleural invasion. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Adenocarcinoma in situ (formerly bronchioloalveolar carcinoma)","B. Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia","C. Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","D. Diffuse alveolar damage"]','C','Adenocarcinoma in situ (AIS), formerly called bronchioloalveolar carcinoma (BAC), is characterized by lepidic growth — tumor cells growing along pre-existing alveolar walls without stromal, vascular, or pleural invasion. It presents as a ground-glass opacity on CT and typically occurs in non-smokers. Complete resection is curative with near 100% 5-year survival when no invasion is present.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Vascular Disease','easy','mcq','The vast majority of pulmonary emboli originate from which anatomical site?','["A. Right atrial thrombi","B. Upper extremity veins from indwelling catheters","C. Pelvic veins in obstetric patients","D. Deep veins of the lower extremities (legs)"]','B','More than 95% of pulmonary emboli originate from thrombi in the deep veins of the lower extremities (deep vein thrombosis). This is stated explicitly in Robbins: "The usual source — thrombi in the deep veins of the leg (>95% of cases)." Less common sources include right atrial thrombi from indwelling catheters.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Vascular Disease','easy','mcq','Pulmonary hypertension is defined as a mean pulmonary artery pressure greater than or equal to:','["A. 15 mmHg at rest","B. 40 mmHg at rest","C. 35 mmHg at rest","D. 25 mmHg at rest"]','D','According to Robbins, pulmonary hypertension is defined as a mean pulmonary artery pressure of 25 mmHg or greater at rest. Normal mean pulmonary artery pressure is 8-20 mmHg. The WHO classifies pulmonary hypertension into 5 groups based on underlying mechanisms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Vascular Disease','medium','mcq','A pulmonary infarct from a pulmonary embolus typically appears hemorrhagic. Why does pulmonary infarction cause hemorrhagic rather than pale (anemic) infarction?','["A. The pulmonary arterial pressure is lower than systemic so collateral flow cannot prevent infarction","B. The pulmonary arteries have high oxygen content making ischemia less likely to cause pale infarction","C. Pulmonary capillaries are fenestrated allowing extravasation of red cells without true infarction","D. Dual blood supply from both pulmonary and bronchial arteries means blood continues to flow into the infarcted area after arterial occlusion"]','A','Pulmonary infarcts are characteristically hemorrhagic because the lung has a dual blood supply (pulmonary arteries from the right heart and bronchial arteries from the aorta). Even after pulmonary artery occlusion, blood from the bronchial circulation continues to flow into the infarcted area, producing the red (hemorrhagic) infarct pattern.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Vascular Disease','medium','mcq','Which finding on histology is most characteristic of advanced (severe) pulmonary arterial hypertension?','["A. Fibrinoid necrosis of pulmonary veins","B. Plexiform lesion in small muscular arteries (40-300 micrometers in diameter)","C. Endothelial desquamation with intimal thickening in large pulmonary arteries","D. Perivascular eosinophilic infiltration with granuloma formation"]','D','The plexiform lesion is the hallmark of severe/advanced pulmonary arterial hypertension. It consists of a network (plexus) of thin-walled channels within a dilated segment of a small muscular pulmonary artery, creating a glomeruloid structure. It represents an extreme form of vascular remodeling due to endothelial proliferation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Vascular Disease','medium','mcq','Idiopathic (primary) pulmonary arterial hypertension is most common in which demographic group?','["A. Women between 20 and 40 years of age","B. Children under 5 years with congenital cardiac defects","C. Men between 60 and 80 years of age","D. Male smokers between 40 and 60 years of age"]','C','Idiopathic pulmonary hypertension is most common in women who are 20 to 40 years of age, as stated in Robbins. It is also seen occasionally in young children. The clinical course is characterized by dyspnea, fatigue, eventual cyanosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, and death from cor pulmonale — usually within 2 to 5 years in 80% of patients without treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Vascular Disease','medium','mcq','In idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension, mutations in which gene are most commonly identified?','["A. TERT (telomerase reverse transcriptase)","B. NF1 (neurofibromin 1)","C. BMPR2 (bone morphogenetic protein receptor type 2)","D. CFTR (cystic fibrosis transmembrane regulator)"]','D','Mutations in BMPR2 (located at the 2q33 locus) are the most common genetic abnormality identified in idiopathic and familial pulmonary arterial hypertension. BMPR2 normally suppresses vascular smooth muscle proliferation; loss of function leads to excessive smooth muscle and endothelial cell proliferation in pulmonary arteries, contributing to vascular remodeling of PAH.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Vascular Disease','medium','mcq','A 52-year-old woman on oral contraceptives develops sudden pleuritic chest pain, hemoptysis, and tachycardia confirmed as pulmonary embolism with infarction. What is the typical radiological appearance of a pulmonary infarct on chest X-ray?','["A. Centrally located round opacification with air bronchograms","B. Bilateral diffuse ground-glass opacities in a bat-wing distribution","C. Unilateral whiteout with mediastinal shift toward the affected side","D. Wedge-shaped peripheral infiltrate with the apex pointing toward the hilum (Hampton''s hump)"]','C','A pulmonary infarct classically appears as a wedge-shaped (pleural-based) infiltrate on chest X-ray, with the apex pointing toward the hilum (Hampton''''s hump sign). This appearance reflects the wedge-shaped distribution of the vascular territory. Robbins notes that infarcts appear 12-36 hours after the event as wedge-shaped infiltrates.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Vascular Disease','hard','mcq','A massive saddle pulmonary embolism causes sudden cardiovascular collapse. What term describes the state where the ECG shows a rhythm but no palpable pulse?','["A. Complete heart block","B. Ventricular fibrillation","C. Pulseless electrical activity (electromechanical dissociation)","D. Torsades de pointes"]','A','Robbins describes that when a patient survives cardiopulmonary resuscitation following a massive embolus, the patient frequently is said to have electromechanical dissociation (now called pulseless electrical activity/PEA) — the ECG shows a rhythm but no pulses are palpated because no blood enters the pulmonary arterial circulation due to obstruction by the embolus.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Vascular Disease','hard','mcq','Which pathologic finding in the pulmonary vasculature would most strongly suggest recurrent pulmonary thromboemboli as the cause of pulmonary hypertension?','["A. Plexiform lesions in small muscular arteries","B. Perivascular lymphocytic infiltration with loss of vascular smooth muscle","C. Multiple organizing or recanalized thrombi in pulmonary arteries","D. Medial hypertrophy with atherosclerosis in large pulmonary arteries"]','D','The presence of many organizing or recanalized thrombi favors recurrent pulmonary emboli as the cause of pulmonary hypertension (WHO Group 4: chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension). In contrast, plexiform lesions are most characteristic of idiopathic (Group 1) pulmonary arterial hypertension. Medial hypertrophy is a general finding in all forms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pulmonary Vascular Disease','hard','mcq','Goodpasture syndrome causes both pulmonary hemorrhage and rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis. Against which antigen are the autoantibodies directed?','["A. Alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen (noncollagenous domain)","B. Myeloperoxidase (MPO-ANCA)","C. Proteinase-3 (PR3-ANCA)","D. Double-stranded DNA (anti-dsDNA)"]','B','In Goodpasture syndrome, circulating autoantibodies are directed against the noncollagenous domain of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen, which is present in both glomerular basement membrane and alveolar basement membrane. This causes rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis and necrotizing hemorrhagic interstitial pneumonitis. Anti-GBM antibodies produce linear IgG deposits on immunofluorescence.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pleural Disease','easy','mcq','Which of the following best distinguishes an exudative pleural effusion from a transudative effusion (Light''''s criteria)?','["A. Pleural fluid protein greater than 3 g/dL or pleural/serum protein ratio greater than 0.5","B. Pleural fluid white cell count greater than 1000 cells per microliter","C. Pleural fluid pH less than 7.2","D. Pleural fluid glucose less than serum glucose"]','D','Light''''s criteria classify pleural effusions as exudates if any one criterion is met: (1) pleural/serum protein ratio >0.5, (2) pleural/serum LDH ratio >0.6, or (3) pleural LDH >2/3 of upper limit of normal serum LDH. A protein >3 g/dL broadly suggests an exudate. Transudates result from hydrostatic/oncotic imbalance; exudates from inflammation or malignancy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pleural Disease','easy','mcq','A spontaneous pneumothorax in an otherwise healthy young adult male is most commonly caused by:','["A. Severe emphysema with multiple bullae","B. Rupture of a subpleural bleb (distal acinar/paraseptal emphysema)","C. Tension pneumothorax from blunt trauma","D. Secondary pneumothorax from Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia"]','D','Spontaneous pneumothorax in young adult males is most commonly caused by rupture of subpleural blebs — typically from distal acinar (paraseptal) emphysema, where enlarged airspaces form adjacent to the pleura at the apex of the lung. This contrasts with secondary pneumothorax, which complicates underlying lung disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pleural Disease','medium','mcq','Malignant mesothelioma is strongly associated with which occupational exposure, and what is its characteristic histologic appearance?','["A. Silica dust exposure; silicotic nodules with whorled hyalinized collagen","B. Asbestos exposure (especially amphibole fibers); biphasic tumor with epithelioid and sarcomatoid components","C. Coal dust exposure; anthracotic macrophage aggregates","D. Beryllium exposure; noncaseating granulomas"]','D','Malignant mesothelioma is strongly associated with asbestos exposure, particularly amphibole fibers (crocidolite). It arises from mesothelial cells lining the pleura. Histologically, it shows a biphasic pattern with epithelioid (tubular/papillary) and sarcomatoid (spindle cell) components. It is IHC-positive for calretinin, WT-1, and D2-40 (podoplanin).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pleural Disease','medium','mcq','Empyema refers to:','["A. Accumulation of pus (infected exudate) in the pleural cavity","B. Fibrinous pleural effusion from rheumatoid arthritis","C. Bloody fluid (hemothorax) in the pleural space from trauma","D. Air in the pleural space causing lung collapse"]','D','Empyema is accumulation of pus (infected exudate) in the pleural cavity, typically resulting from bacterial pneumonia, lung abscess, or thoracic surgery. It presents as a turbid, foul-smelling pleural fluid with high WBC count (predominantly neutrophils), low pH, and low glucose. Drainage and antibiotics are the cornerstones of treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pleural Disease','medium','mcq','Which of the following conditions would most likely produce a transudative pleural effusion?','["A. Malignant mesothelioma","B. Congestive heart failure","C. Bacterial pneumonia with parapneumonic effusion","D. Rheumatoid arthritis with pleuritis"]','A','Transudative pleural effusions result from altered hydrostatic or oncotic pressures without pleural disease: causes include congestive heart failure (increased hydrostatic pressure), hypoalbuminemia (decreased oncotic pressure), and cirrhosis. In contrast, exudative effusions result from pleural inflammation or malignancy (pneumonia, mesothelioma, rheumatoid arthritis).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pleural Disease','medium','mcq','Which type of pneumothorax causes mediastinal shift toward the unaffected side and is a life-threatening emergency?','["A. Tension pneumothorax","B. Open (sucking chest wound) pneumothorax","C. Simple (closed) pneumothorax","D. Spontaneous pneumothorax from bleb rupture"]','A','Tension pneumothorax occurs when air enters the pleural space through a one-way valve mechanism. Each breath draws more air in without escape. Intrapleural pressure rises above atmospheric, causing mediastinal shift away from the affected side, compressing the contralateral lung and great veins, reducing cardiac output. It is a life-threatening emergency requiring immediate needle thoracocentesis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pleural Disease','medium','mcq','Malignant mesothelioma has a characteristic latency period. What is the typical time from asbestos exposure to development of mesothelioma?','["A. 25-45 years","B. 10-15 years","C. Over 60 years","D. 1-5 years"]','D','Malignant mesothelioma typically has a very long latency period of 25-45 years from asbestos exposure to tumor development. This extreme latency makes it challenging to link clinical presentation to occupational exposure history, particularly as regulations restricting asbestos use were only implemented in many countries in the 1970s-80s.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pleural Disease','hard','mcq','Which feature is most specific to rheumatoid pleural effusion?','["A. Exudative effusion with predominantly lymphocytic cells and elevated ADA","B. Transudative effusion with normal glucose and LDH","C. Exudative effusion with very low glucose (often less than 30 mg/dL) and low complement levels","D. Bloody exudative effusion with positive cytology for multinucleated giant cells"]','B','Rheumatoid pleural effusions are characteristically exudative with very low glucose (often less than 30 mg/dL — lower than in any other cause of pleural effusion), low complement, and elevated LDH. The low glucose results from impaired glucose transport across the inflamed pleura. Cholesterol crystals may also be present.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pleural Disease','hard','mcq','A 68-year-old male with a 40-year history of shipbuilding presents with unilateral pleural thickening encasing the lung and recurrent pleural effusions. Biopsy shows spindle cells and epithelioid cells with positive calretinin and WT-1 staining. Which statement about his condition is correct?','["A. It is a primary pleural adenocarcinoma metastasizing from lung","B. It is benign pleural fibrosis from asbestos causing restrictive physiology only","C. It is solitary fibrous tumor of the pleura, which is resectable and curable","D. It is malignant mesothelioma with a biphasic pattern; median survival is 9-12 months"]','B','This is classic malignant mesothelioma — asbestos exposure, long latency, encasement of lung, positive calretinin and WT-1 (mesothelial markers). Malignant mesothelioma has a very poor prognosis with median survival of 9-12 months from diagnosis. The biphasic pattern (epithelioid + sarcomatoid) is characteristic. Solitary fibrous tumor is CD34+ and usually benign.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Pleural Disease','hard','mcq','In compression atelectasis caused by a large pleural effusion, the mediastinum shifts in which direction?','["A. Toward the affected (ipsilateral) side","B. No mediastinal shift occurs","C. Downward toward the diaphragm","D. Away from the affected (contralateral) side"]','C','In compression atelectasis, accumulation of fluid, tumor, or air within the pleural cavity causes the lung to collapse by external compression. The mediastinum shifts away from the affected side (toward the contralateral side), because the expanding pleural collection pushes mediastinal structures. This distinguishes compression atelectasis from resorption atelectasis, where the mediastinum shifts toward the atelectatic lung.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Neonatal & Paediatric Respiratory','easy','mcq','Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (hyaline membrane disease) is caused primarily by:','["A. Deficiency of surfactant leading to increased alveolar surface tension and atelectasis","B. Meconium aspiration causing chemical pneumonitis","C. Congenital diaphragmatic hernia causing lung compression","D. Group B Streptococcal pneumonia acquired during vaginal delivery"]','C','Neonatal RDS (hyaline membrane disease) is caused by surfactant deficiency, primarily in preterm infants. Surfactant reduces alveolar surface tension; its absence leads to increased surface tension, alveolar collapse (atelectasis), ventilation-perfusion mismatch, hypoxemia, and hyaline membrane formation from fibrin-rich edema fluid mixed with necrotic epithelial cell remnants.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Neonatal & Paediatric Respiratory','easy','mcq','Hyaline membranes in neonatal RDS are composed of which material?','["A. Calcified cartilaginous fragments from immature bronchi","B. Amyloid deposited from maternal circulation crossing the placenta","C. Mucopolysaccharide secreted by goblet cells in immature airways","D. Fibrin-rich edema fluid mixed with cytoplasmic and lipid remnants of necrotic epithelial cells"]','B','Hyaline membranes in neonatal RDS (and adult ARDS) consist of fibrin-rich edema fluid mixed with cytoplasmic and lipid remnants of necrotic alveolar epithelial cells. They line the distended respiratory bronchioles and alveolar ducts as waxy, eosinophilic membranes, giving the lungs their characteristic glassy appearance on histology.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Neonatal & Paediatric Respiratory','medium','mcq','An infant born at 32 weeks develops respiratory distress within 4 hours of birth. The lungs appear airless and reddish-purple, similar in color to the liver. What is the mechanism of surfactant deficiency in premature infants?','["A. Mutations in SP-B gene causing complete absence of surfactant protein B","B. Immaturity of type II pneumocytes which do not produce adequate surfactant until late in gestation","C. Maternal corticosteroid therapy suppressing surfactant production by negative feedback","D. Meconium coating alveoli and inactivating surfactant function"]','A','Type II pneumocytes, which synthesize and secrete surfactant, are not functionally mature until approximately 35 weeks of gestation. Premature infants lack sufficient mature type II cells and have inadequate surfactant reserves. Antenatal maternal corticosteroids accelerate type II cell maturation. Grossly, the lungs are solid, airless, reddish-purple (like liver), and sink in water.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Neonatal & Paediatric Respiratory','medium','mcq','Infants of diabetic mothers have a higher risk of developing RDS despite adequate gestational age. What is the mechanism?','["A. High fetal insulin levels counteract the effects of cortisol, suppressing surfactant synthesis","B. Hyperglycemia directly damages type II pneumocytes in utero","C. Placental dysfunction reduces glucocorticoid transfer to the fetus","D. Maternal hyperglycemia depletes phospholipid precursors needed for surfactant production"]','A','In infants of diabetic mothers, the fetus is exposed to high maternal blood glucose, causing fetal hyperglycemia and compensatory fetal hyperinsulinemia. High insulin levels suppress the cortisol-stimulated surfactant synthesis by type II pneumocytes, increasing the risk of RDS. Labor normally increases surfactant synthesis, so cesarean section before labor onset also increases RDS risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Neonatal & Paediatric Respiratory','medium','mcq','Which clinical feature distinguishes croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) from epiglottitis in a child?','["A. Croup is diagnosed by direct laryngoscopy showing a cherry-red epiglottis; epiglottitis shows subglottic narrowing on X-ray","B. Croup causes inspiratory stridor with a barking cough due to parainfluenza virus; epiglottitis causes a toxic-appearing child with muffled voice and drooling, typically due to H. influenzae type b","C. Croup affects older teenagers; epiglottitis primarily affects infants under 6 months","D. Croup causes high fever >40 degrees C and drooling; epiglottitis causes mild fever with barking cough"]','D','Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) is caused by parainfluenza virus, affects children 6 months to 3 years, and produces a characteristic barking cough and inspiratory stridor from subglottic edema (steeple sign on X-ray). Epiglottitis, caused by H. influenzae type b (now rare with HiB vaccine), causes rapid-onset high fever, drooling, muffled voice, and tripod posture, and is a life-threatening airway emergency.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Neonatal & Paediatric Respiratory','medium','mcq','Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD) is a complication of:','["A. Meconium aspiration syndrome in post-term infants","B. Mechanical ventilation and oxygen therapy for neonatal RDS, leading to chronic lung injury","C. Congenital cytomegalovirus infection causing chronic interstitial pneumonitis","D. Pulmonary hypoplasia from oligohydramnios and congenital diaphragmatic hernia"]','B','Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD) is a chronic lung disease of prematurity that develops as a complication of the treatment of neonatal RDS — mechanical ventilation with high pressures (barotrauma), high oxygen concentrations (oxygen toxicity), and repeated infections lead to lung injury, inflammation, and disordered lung development.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Neonatal & Paediatric Respiratory','medium','mcq','Which hormone plays the most important role in stimulating fetal lung maturation and surfactant production?','["A. Thyroid hormone (thyroxine)","B. Glucocorticoids (cortisol)","C. Insulin","D. Prolactin"]','D','Glucocorticoids (cortisol) are the most important hormones stimulating surfactant synthesis and type II pneumocyte maturation. Conditions increasing cortisol (intrauterine stress, FGR) lower the risk of RDS. Antenatal corticosteroids (betamethasone/dexamethasone) given to mothers at risk of preterm delivery are the most effective intervention to reduce RDS severity. Insulin suppresses this effect.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Neonatal & Paediatric Respiratory','hard','mcq','A neonate with RDS survives with mechanical ventilation. In the organizing phase of RDS/DAD, what cellular event is critical for lung recovery?','["A. Proliferation of type I pneumocytes restoring the alveolar surface","B. Proliferation of type II pneumocytes which replace injured type I cells and give rise to new type I cells","C. Proliferation of goblet cells filling alveoli with protective mucus","D. Fibroblast-driven scarring replacing all alveolar epithelium with fibrous tissue"]','A','In the organizing phase of RDS (and ARDS/DAD), type II pneumocytes proliferate to replace the injured alveolar epithelium. Type II cells are the regenerative reservoir for the alveolar epithelium — they can proliferate and differentiate into type I pneumocytes, which cover 95% of the alveolar surface. Type I cells themselves lack proliferative capacity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Neonatal & Paediatric Respiratory','hard','mcq','A mutation in the ABCA3 gene causes surfactant dysfunction disorder. What is the function of ABCA3 protein?','["A. ABCA3 is an ATP-binding cassette protein involved in lipid trafficking into lamellar bodies for surfactant packaging and secretion","B. ABCA3 is a chloride channel on apical surface of airway epithelium essential for mucociliary clearance","C. ABCA3 is a receptor for cortisol mediating surfactant gene transcription in type II cells","D. ABCA3 is a phospholipase that generates phosphatidylcholine for surfactant synthesis"]','B','ABCA3 (ATP-binding cassette protein member A3) is the most frequently mutated gene in surfactant dysfunction disorders, causing an autosomal recessive disorder. ABCA3 functions as a lipid transporter involved in trafficking phospholipids into lamellar bodies of type II pneumocytes for surfactant packaging and secretion. Mutations disrupt this process, causing surfactant deficiency presenting in the first few months of life.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Pathology','Neonatal & Paediatric Respiratory','hard','mcq','Which intervention has most dramatically reduced mortality from neonatal RDS, decreasing deaths from approximately 5000 per year to fewer than 900 per year?','["A. Universal cesarean section delivery before 36 weeks of gestation","B. High-frequency oscillatory ventilation replacing conventional mechanical ventilation","C. Antenatal corticosteroids and postnatal exogenous surfactant replacement therapy","D. Erythropoietin administration to stimulate oxygen-carrying capacity"]','A','The dramatic reduction in RDS mortality is primarily attributed to two interventions: (1) antenatal corticosteroids (betamethasone/dexamethasone) given to mothers at risk of preterm delivery to accelerate fetal lung maturation, and (2) postnatal exogenous surfactant replacement therapy (natural or synthetic surfactant administered via endotracheal tube) to correct the primary surfactant deficiency. Robbins cites these improvements as responsible for the sharp decrease in RDS deaths.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
